The '''2014 UEFA Champions League final''' was the final match of the [[2013–14 UEFA Champions League]], the 59th season of Europe's premier club [[association football|football]] tournament organised by [[UEFA]], and the 22nd season since it was renamed from the European Champion Clubs' Cup to the [[UEFA Champions League]].

The match took place on Saturday, 24 May 2014, at the [[Estádio da Luz]] in [[Lisbon]], Portugal, between Spanish sides [[Real Madrid CF|Real Madrid]] and [[Atlético Madrid]]. It was the fifth tournament final to feature two teams from the same association, the second all-Spanish final and the first between teams from the same city. Real Madrid won the match 4–1 after extra time, a wonder 93rd-minute header by [[Sergio Ramos]], which cancelled out [[Diego Godín]]'s first-half goal saved Real Madrid CF from defeat. In extra time with goals from [[Gareth Bale]], [[Marcelo (footballer, born 1988)|Marcelo]] and [[Cristiano Ronaldo]] they won the Champions league. In doing so, Real Madrid secured a record 10th title (La Décima), 12 years after their ninth title.

As the winners, Real Madrid earned the right to play against [[2013–14 UEFA Europa League]] winners [[Sevilla FC|Sevilla]] in the [[2014 UEFA Super Cup]]. They also qualified to enter the semi-finals of the [[2014 FIFA Club World Cup]] as the UEFA representative, ultimately triumphing in both competitions. The match is considered one of the greatest Champions League finals in history by the media.<ref>{{Cite web |date=8 June 2023|title=Every Champions League final, ranked from worst to best |url=https://www.goal.com/en/lists/ranked-every-champions-league-final-from-worst-to-best/14b424ryiryj21ptuaotpj1ha5 |access-date=1 November 2024|website=Goal.com}}</ref>

==Venue==
[[File:Estadio da Luz 2012.jpg|thumbnail|left|The [[Estádio da Luz]] hosted the European Cup/Champions League final for the first time.]]
The [[Estádio da Luz]] (officially known as the Estádio do Sport Lisboa e Benfica) in [[Lisbon]], Portugal, was chosen as the venue of the 2014 UEFA Champions League final at a UEFA Executive Committee meeting in [[Istanbul]], Turkey, on 20 March 2012.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.uefa.com/insideuefa/mediaservices/mediareleases/newsid=1766100.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200621175401/https://www.uefa.com/insideuefa/mediaservices/mediareleases/newsid=1766100.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=21 June 2020 |title=UEFA Executive Committee agenda for Istanbul meeting |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=9 March 2012|access-date=21 June 2020}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Lisbon to stage 2014 UEFA Champions League final |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/01fb-0e7d59a108bb-e85a09cb5276-1000--lisbon-to-stage-2014-uefa-champions-league-final/ |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=20 March 2012|access-date=21 June 2020}}</ref>

The home stadium of Portuguese Primeira Liga side [[S.L. Benfica|Benfica]] since 2003, it was newly built to host five matches of [[UEFA Euro 2004]], including the [[UEFA Euro 2004 final|final]]. Before the old stadium's demolition in 2003, to make way for the new 65,000-capacity ground, the [[Estádio da Luz (1954)|original Estádio da Luz]] hosted the [[1992 European Cup Winners' Cup final]], where [[SV Werder Bremen|Werder Bremen]] beat [[AS Monaco FC|Monaco]] 2–0, and the second leg of the [[1983 UEFA Cup final]], where [[R.S.C. Anderlecht|Anderlecht]] secured a 1–1 draw with Benfica to lift the trophy.<ref name="previous finals">{{cite web |title=Finals in Lisbon |url=http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/newsid=2086381.html |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations|date=1 April 2014|url-status=dead|archive-date=2 May 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140502114613/http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/newsid=2086381.html}}</ref>

The last time the European Cup final was played in Lisbon was in [[1967 European Cup final|1967]], when Scottish side [[Celtic F.C.|Celtic]] beat [[Inter Milan]] of Italy 2–1 at the [[Estádio Nacional]]. The Portuguese capital also hosted the [[2005 UEFA Cup final]] at the [[Estádio José Alvalade]], home of Benfica's local rivals and finalists [[Sporting CP]], who lost 3–1 to [[PFC CSKA Moscow|CSKA Moscow]].<ref name="previous finals"/>

==Background==
[[File:Carlo Ancelotti 2012-01-02 (1).jpg|thumb|[[Carlo Ancelotti]] was managing in his fourth Champions League final, having won two of three previous finals with [[A.C. Milan]].]]

This was the first final in the history of the competition to be disputed by two teams from the same city. It was also the second all-Spanish final, after the [[2000 UEFA Champions League final|2000 final]] between Real Madrid and [[Valencia CF|Valencia]], and the fifth final between teams from the same country, the others being [[2003 UEFA Champions League final|2003]] (Italy), [[2008 UEFA Champions League final|2008]] (England), and [[2013 UEFA Champions League final|2013]] (Germany).<ref>{{cite web |title=One-nation UEFA Champions League finals |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/025a-0ea7b0821ac4-d0cf21e6cd1f-1000--one-nation-uefa-champions-league-finals/ |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations|date=5 May 2014 }}</ref>

[[File:Diego Simeone - 01.jpg|thumb|left|150px|[[Diego Simeone]] reached his first UEFA Champions League final as a manager with Atlético Madrid.]]

[[Real Madrid CF|Real Madrid]] reached a record 13th final after a 5–0 aggregate win against defending champions [[FC Bayern Munich|Bayern Munich]], making it the first time the club had reached the final since they won their [[European Cup and UEFA Champions League records and statistics|record ninth title]] in 2002.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/0/football/27193338 |title=Bayern Munich 0 Real Madrid 4 |date=29 April 2014 |work=BBC Sport |access-date=30 April 2014 }}</ref> Previously they won finals in [[1956 European Cup final|1956]], [[1957 European Cup final|1957]], [[1958 European Cup final|1958]], [[1959 European Cup final|1959]], [[1960 European Cup final|1960]], [[1966 European Cup final|1966]], [[1998 UEFA Champions League final|1998]], [[2000 UEFA Champions League final|2000]], and [[2002 UEFA Champions League final|2002]], and lost in [[1962 European Cup final|1962]], [[1964 European Cup final|1964]], and [[1981 European Cup final|1981]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Madrid's European Cup finals |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0250-0c51077e88f5-16d1b98c88fc-1000--madrid-s-european-cup-finals/ |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations|date=29 April 2014 }}</ref> This was also their 17th final in all UEFA club competitions, having also played in two [[UEFA Cup Winners' Cup|Cup Winners' Cup]] finals (losing in [[1971 European Cup Winners' Cup final|1971]] and [[1983 European Cup Winners' Cup final|1983]]) and two [[UEFA Europa League|UEFA Cup]] finals (winning in [[1985 UEFA Cup final|1985]] and [[1986 UEFA Cup final|1986]]).<ref>{{cite web |url=http://theworldgame.sbs.com.au/news/1188038/ucl-fast-facts:-bayern-munich-v-real-madrid |title=UCL fast facts: Bayern Munich v Real Madrid |publisher=SBS |date=30 April 2014 |access-date=1 May 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140502033018/http://theworldgame.sbs.com.au/news/1188038/ucl-fast-facts:-bayern-munich-v-real-madrid |archive-date=2 May 2014 |url-status=dead }}</ref> It was the fourth Champions League final for their current coach [[Carlo Ancelotti]], who previously coached [[A.C. Milan]] to victories in [[2003 UEFA Champions League final|2003]] and [[2007 UEFA Champions League final|2007]] and defeat in [[2005 UEFA Champions League final|2005]], equalling the [[European Cup and UEFA Champions League records and statistics#Managers|record]] shared by [[Miguel Muñoz]], [[Marcello Lippi]] and [[Alex Ferguson]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.realmadrid.com/en/news/2014/04/fourth-champions-league-final-for-ancelotti?pid=1383833298379|title=Fourth Champions League final for Ancelotti|publisher=Real Madrid|date=30 April 2014}}</ref> He joined [[Bob Paisley]] as the [[List of European Cup and UEFA Champions League winning managers#Managers with multiple titles|only manager to have won three titles]],<ref>{{cite web |title=UEFA Champions League final facts and figures |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/025a-0ea7bd148aee-d58743bef67d-1000--uefa-champions-league-final-facts-and-figures/ |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations|date=7 May 2014 }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Paisley's European Cup legacy |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/022a-0e93411c2afb-540d557c72f0-1000--paisley-s-european-cup-legacy-at-liverpool/ |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations|date=19 May 2014 }}</ref> and also became the fifth manager to win titles with two clubs, after [[Ernst Happel]], [[Ottmar Hitzfeld]], [[José Mourinho]] and [[Jupp Heynckes]].<ref name="stats">{{cite web |title=Semi-finals: second-leg stats and facts |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/025a-0ea7bd148aed-4abc40b97c9a-1000--semi-finals-second-leg-stats-and-facts/ |publisher=Union of European Football Associations|date=1 May 2014 }}</ref>

[[Atlético Madrid]], who a week earlier had won their first [[La Liga]] title since 1996, reached their second European Cup final, 40 years after their first, after defeating 2012 champions [[Chelsea F.C.|Chelsea]] 3–1 on aggregate.<ref>{{cite news|title=Chelsea 1 Atlético Madrid 3|url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/0/football/27210847|work=BBC Sport|access-date=30 April 2014}}</ref> This was the longest wait between finals, eclipsing the 38-year wait by [[Inter Milan]] (1972–2010).<ref>{{cite web|url=http://theworldgame.sbs.com.au/news/1188101/UCL-fast-facts-Chelsea-v-Atletico-Madrid|title=UCL fast facts: Chelsea v Atletico Madrid|publisher=[[SBS (Australian TV channel)|SBS]]|date=1 May 2014|access-date=1 May 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140502040538/http://theworldgame.sbs.com.au/news/1188101/UCL-fast-facts-Chelsea-v-Atletico-Madrid|archive-date=2 May 2014|url-status=dead}}</ref> Atlético Madrid's only previous European Cup final in [[1974 European Cup final|1974]] ended in defeat to [[FC Bayern Munich|Bayern Munich]] after a replay.<ref>{{cite web |title=Atlético's previous European Cup final |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0214-0e8a74357a25-77cb5215264f-1000--atletico-s-previous-european-cup-final/ |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations|date=30 April 2014 }}</ref> Atlético Madrid have also played in three [[UEFA Cup Winners' Cup|Cup Winners' Cup]] finals (winning in [[1962 European Cup Winners' Cup final|1962]], and losing in [[1963 European Cup Winners' Cup final|1963]] and [[1986 European Cup Winners' Cup final|1986]]) and two [[UEFA Europa League|Europa League]] finals (winning in [[2010 UEFA Europa League final|2010]] and [[2012 UEFA Europa League final|2012]]), with their most recent Europa League triumph in 2012 led by current coach [[Diego Simeone]]. He had the chance to join fellow Argentinians [[Luis Carniglia]] and [[Helenio Herrera]] as the [[List of European Cup and UEFA Champions League winning managers#By nationality|only non-European coaches]] to win the European Cup/Champions League.<ref>{{cite web |title=Simeone aims to join Argentina's winners |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/025a-0ea7ba676fcb-1a43ba482937-1000--simeone-aims-to-join-argentina-s-winners/ |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations|date=12 May 2014 }}</ref> Had they won the final, they would have become the first Spanish club and the fifth club overall to [[UEFA club competition records and statistics#List of teams to have won the main European club competitions|have won all three pre-1999 major European trophies]] ([[UEFA Champions League|European Cup/Champions League]], UEFA Cup/Europa League and the now-defunct Cup Winners' Cup).

The only previous [[Madrid derby]] matches in European competitions were in the [[1958–59 European Cup]] semi-finals, where Real Madrid defeated Atlético Madrid 2–1 in a replay, after a 2–2 aggregate draw.<ref>{{cite web |title=Fancy meeting you here: Europe's local derbies |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefasupercup/news/0250-0c511a17f2c9-cd9f64053242-1000--fancy-meeting-you-here-local-derbies-in-europe/ |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations|date=30 April 2014 }}</ref> In the 2013–14 season, Atlético Madrid defeated Real Madrid 1–0 away and drew 2–2 at home in [[2013–14 La Liga|La Liga]], while Real Madrid eliminated Atlético Madrid in the [[2013–14 Copa del Rey|Copa del Rey]] semi-finals, winning 3–0 at home and 2–0 away.<ref>{{cite web |title=Match Press Kit |url=http://www.uefa.org/mediaservices/presskits/uefachampionsleague/season=2014/round=2000483/day=13/session=1/match=2011883/index.html |publisher=UEFA.org (Union of European Football Associations) |date=24 May 2014 |access-date=26 May 2014 }}</ref>

