==Pre-match==
===Ambassador===
[[File:Luís Figo arriving at IKA Tehran Airport.jpg|thumbnail|upright|[[Luís Figo]], ambassador of the 2014 UEFA Champions League final]]

Former Portugal international player [[Luís Figo]], who won the Champions League with [[Real Madrid CF|Real Madrid]] in 2002, was named as the ambassador for the final.<ref>{{cite web |title=Figo embarks on ambassadorial role |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0253-0d0408001718-99d1eacd8ea8-1000--figo-embarks-on-ambassadorial-role/ |publisher=UEFA.com (Union of European Football Associations) |date=29 August 2013 }}</ref>

===Logo===
UEFA unveiled the visual identity of the final on 29 August 2013, the same day as the group stage draw. The design concept was inspired by elements from the [[Portuguese discoveries]], namely the [[armillary sphere]] and the [[Compass rose|windrose]], which were important instruments used by Portuguese sea explorers to measure the position of stars.<ref>{{cite web |title=Lisbon visual identity unveiled |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0253-0d0407a273b0-7cb9ad24c48a-1000--lisbon-visual-identity-unveiled/ |publisher=UEFA.com (Union of European Football Associations) |date=29 August 2013 }}</ref>

===Ticketing===
The international ticket sales phase for the general public ran from 10 to 21 March 2014. Tickets were available in four price categories: [[Euro|€]]390, €280, €160, and €70.<ref>{{cite web |title=Lisbon final tickets go on international sale |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0213-0ea1488fd4c6-ab7cceeb3a8c-1000--lisbon-final-tickets-go-on-international-sale/ |publisher=UEFA.com (Union of European Football Associations) |date=10 March 2014 }}</ref>

The two finalist clubs were each allocated 16,970 tickets by UEFA. Atlético made 14,000 tickets available to club members, with a limit of one ticket per member.<ref>{{cite web|title=Reparto de entradas para la final de la UEFA Champions League|url=http://clubatleticodemadrid.com/noticias/reparto-de-entradas-para-la-final-de-la-uefa-champions-league|publisher=Club Atlético de Madrid|access-date=25 May 2014|language=es|date=3 May 2014}}</ref> Real Madrid received 24,103 requests from 73,314 club members for a total of 13,134 tickets; due to the high demand, the tickets were awarded by means of a draw.<ref>{{cite web|title=Real Madrid has held its draw for Champions League final tickets|url=http://www.realmadrid.com/en/news/2014/05/real-madrid-has-held-its-draw-for-champions-league-final-tickets|publisher=Real Madrid|access-date=25 May 2014|date=6 May 2014}}</ref>

===Officials===
[[File:Björn Kuipers - ConfedCup2013Champions15 (cropped).jpg|thumb|160px|UEFA announced [[Björn Kuipers]] as the referee for the final on 7 May.]]

Dutch referee [[Björn Kuipers]] was named by UEFA on 7 May 2014 as the referee for the final.<ref>{{cite web |title=Kuipers handed UEFA Champions League final |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0215-0e1586300403-450fd1c05a3d-1000--kuipers-handed-uefa-champions-league-final/ |publisher=UEFA.com (Union of European Football Associations) |date=7 May 2014 }}</ref> He has previously taken charge of the [[2011 UEFA Super Cup]], the [[2013 UEFA Europa League final]], and the [[2013 FIFA Confederations Cup final]]. He became the fourth Dutch referee in a European Cup/Champions League final, after [[Leo Horn]] (1957, 1962), [[Charles Corver]] (1978), and [[Dick Jol]] (2001). The rest of the refereeing team are fellow countrymen Sander van Roekel and Erwin Zeinstra as assistant referees, [[Pol van Boekel]] and Richard Liesveld as additional assistant referees, Angelo Boonman as reserve assistant referee, and Turkey's [[Cüneyt Çakır]] as the fourth official.

===Related events===
The UEFA Champions League and UEFA Women's Champions League trophies were handed over to the host city of Lisbon at a ceremony held at the City Hall, on 17 April 2014. The Mayor of Lisbon [[António Costa]] received the silverware from the hands of UEFA President [[Michel Platini]], who justified the decision to stage the 2014 UEFA Champions League showpiece match in Lisbon with the fact that it had "been too long since the final had been in Portugal" and for "the passion and love of football the Portuguese have."<ref name="trophies handover">{{cite web|last=Crompton|first=Sam|title=Lisbon receives Champions League trophies|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0214-0e180a2b86e7-e11601882176-1000--lisbon-receives-champions-league-trophies/|publisher=UEFA.com (Union of European Football Associations)|access-date=27 May 2014|date=17 April 2014}}</ref> The title holders of both competitions were represented at the event by Bayern Munich chairman [[Karl-Heinz Rummenigge]] and midfielder [[Xherdan Shaqiri]], and [[VfL Wolfsburg (women)|Wolfsburg]] defender Lina Magull. Also in attendance were [[Portuguese Football Federation]] (FPF) President Fernando Gomes and final ambassador Luís Figo. Upon their arrival to Lisbon and before the ceremony, the trophies were paraded by old tram through the city in the hands of trophy tour ambassadors [[Vítor Baía]], former Portuguese international and [[FC Porto|Porto]] goalkeeper, and Mónica Jorge, former coach of the women's national team.<ref name="trophies handover"/>

The annual UEFA Champions Festival took place from 22 to 25 May 2014 at [[Praça do Comércio]] in the city centre.<ref>{{cite web |title=Champions Festival opens in Lisbon on 22 May |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0214-0e157b2b1b35-9248130637f4-1000--champions-festival-opens-in-lisbon-on-22-may/ |publisher=UEFA.com (Union of European Football Associations) |date=15 April 2014 |access-date=27 May 2014}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|title=Schedule announced for UEFA Champions Festival|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0215-0e8adb8a8b6e-e04b22f0f783-1000--schedule-announced-for-uefa-champions-festival/|publisher=UEFA.com (Union of European Football Associations)|access-date=27 May 2014|date=16 May 2014}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|last=Pimentel|first=José Nuno|title=Gallery, museum openings begin Lisbon 'festivities'|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/025a-0ea7b1120586-cbd9f24f4d11-1000--gallery-museum-openings-begin-lisbon-festivities/|publisher=UEFA.com (Union of European Football Associations)|access-date=27 May 2014|date=6 May 2014}}</ref>

The [[2014 UEFA Women's Champions League final]] was held on 22 May 2014 at [[Estádio do Restelo]],<ref>{{cite web |title=Estádio do Restelo stages Lisbon final |url=https://www.uefa.com/womenschampionsleague/news/020f-0e146c06f43a-acb054f4f1ad-1000--estadio-do-restelo-stages-lisbon-final/ |publisher=UEFA.com (Union of European Football Associations) |date=8 November 2013 |access-date=27 May 2014}}</ref> featuring Swedish side [[Tyresö FF|Tyresö]] and defending champions [[VfL Wolfsburg (women)|Wolfsburg]]. Losing 2–0 at half-time, Wolfsburg made a comeback to win the match 4–3. [[Martina Müller (footballer)|Martina Müller]], who scored the winner in the [[2013 UEFA Women's Champions League final|previous final]], repeated the feat to secure the German team's second consecutive title.<ref>{{cite web|last=Saffer|first=Paul|title=Müller the hero again as Wolfsburg win classic final|url=https://www.uefa.com/womenschampionsleague/news/0215-0e15e0212966-4d5823ff01ad-1000--muller-the-hero-again-as-wolfsburg-win-classic-final/|publisher=UEFA.com (Union of European Football Associations)|access-date=27 May 2014|date=22 May 2014}}</ref>

===Opening ceremony===
[[File:Final Champions League 2014 Lesboa.jpg|thumb|Opening ceremony]]
The ceremony preceding the kick-off was organised by Canadian company Circo de Bakuza, with artistic direction by London-based choreographer Wanda Rokicki.<ref name="Global News">{{cite web|last=Lau|first=Rachel|title=A look at the Montreal company behind the Champions League opening ceremony|url=http://globalnews.ca/news/1352559/a-look-at-the-montreal-company-behind-the-champions-league-opening-ceremony/|publisher=[[Global News]]|access-date=26 May 2014|date=25 May 2014}}</ref> She was responsible for the artistic segments of large international sporting events, such as the [[2004 Summer Olympics]], the [[2010 FIFA World Cup]], and the [[2002 Commonwealth Games|2002]] and [[2006 Commonwealth Games]].<ref name="Publico">{{cite web|last=Ribeiro|first=João|title=A "versão artística" de Portugal vai entrar em campo na Luz|trans-title=The 'artistical version' of Portugal will come onto the pitch at the Estádio da Luz|url=http://www.publico.pt/desporto/noticia/a-versao-artistica-de-portugal-vai-estar-em-campo-na-luz-1632524|publisher=[[Público (Portugal)|Público]]|access-date=26 May 2014|language=pt|date=17 April 2014}}</ref>
Conceived "to honor Portuguese tradition, including naval history or the art of tiling",<ref name="Publico"/> the show required six months of preparation and involved a total of 400 volunteers, 90 singers and 84 large-sized banners.<ref name="Global News"/>
The [[UEFA Champions League anthem]] was interpreted by Portuguese [[fado]] singer [[Mariza]].<ref>{{cite web|last=Pietra|first=Hugo|title=Mariza brings fado to Lisbon final|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/025a-0ea7adbc2fde-e5eb4c4c6af7-1000--mariza-brings-fado-to-lisbon-final/|publisher=UEFA.com (Union of European Football Associations)|access-date=26 May 2014|date=24 May 2014}}</ref>

==Match==

===Team selection===
The only player suspended from the final was Real Madrid's [[Xabi Alonso]], who picked up his third booking of the competition in the second leg of the semi-final.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.caughtoffside.com/2014/05/01/real-madrids-xabi-alonso-and-the-21-other-players-to-miss-the-champions-league-final-via-suspension/|title=Real Madrid's Xabi Alonso And The 21 Other Players To Miss The Champions League Final Via Suspension|publisher=caughtoffside.com|date=1 May 2014}}</ref> In his place, Carlo Ancelotti selected German midfielder [[Sami Khedira]], who himself had only recently returned from injury. [[Pepe (footballer, born 1983)|Pepe]] was also left out of the starting XI, with 21-year-old French centre-back [[Raphaël Varane]] playing instead. Real Madrid's front three of [[Cristiano Ronaldo]], [[Gareth Bale]] and [[Karim Benzema]] were originally doubtful for the match, but all three overcame injuries to start the match.<ref>{{cite news |title=Real Madrid's Cristiano Ronaldo and Gareth Bale fit for Champions League final |url=http://www1.skysports.com/football/news/11835/9318851/real-madrids-cristiano-ronaldo-and-gareth-bale-fit-for-champions-league-final |publisher=Sky Sports (BSkyB) |date=21 May 2014 |access-date=25 May 2014 }}</ref>

Atlético captain [[Gabi (footballer, born 1983)|Gabi]] returned from a one-match European suspension, while striker [[Diego Costa]] was included in the starting line-up, having undergone horse placenta treatment for a hamstring injury suffered in the last league match the previous Saturday.<ref>{{cite news |first=Dermot |last=Corrigan |title=Costa 'using horse placenta treatment' |url=http://www.espnfc.com/news/story/_/id/1828590/atletico-madrid-striker-diego-costa-using-horse-placenta-bid-make-champions-league-final-reports?cc=5739 |work=ESPN FC |publisher=ESPN Internet Ventures |date=22 May 2014 |access-date=25 May 2014 }}</ref> However, despite initial optimism, key player [[Arda Turan]] did not recover in time after suffering an injury in the same game.<ref>{{cite news |title=La Liga: Atletico Madrid worried by Diego Costa, Arda Turan injuries |url=http://www1.skysports.com/FIFA-World-Cup-2014/news/12024/9315964/la-liga-atletico-madrid-worried-by-diego-costa-arda-turan-injuries |publisher=Sky Sports (BSkyB) |date=18 May 2014 |access-date=25 May 2014 }}</ref>

===Kits===
Atlético Madrid wore shirts with the name of their former player and manager [[Luis Aragonés]] inside the collar following his death in February 2014, with the symbolism approved by UEFA President [[Michel Platini]].<ref>{{cite news |first=Luis |last=Aznar |title=El Atlético llevará el nombre de Luis Aragones en las camisetas de la final |trans-title=Atlético will wear the name of Luis Aragonés in their shirts for the final |url=http://www.marca.com/2014/05/22/futbol/champions_league/final-champions/1400768288.html |newspaper=[[Marca (newspaper)|Marca]] |date=22 May 2014 |access-date=22 May 2014 }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Atlético's Gabi going all out to honour Aragonés |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/025a-0ea7adbc2fdd-7aa0437e6449-1000--atletico-s-gabi-going-all-out-to-honour-aragones/ |publisher=UEFA.com (Union of European Football Associations) |date=23 May 2014 }}</ref> Both teams wore their home kits for the final, as they do in domestic meetings.<ref>{{cite web |title=Atlético to wear home kit for final |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0253-0d7f36dad6ce-fb4d6d6c33d5-1000--atletico-to-wear-home-kit-for-final/ |publisher=UEFA.com (Union of European Football Associations) |date=23 May 2014 }}</ref>

===Summary===
[[File:Final Champions League 2014 Real Madrid - Atlético de Madrid (14081181609).jpg|thumb|Real Madrid celebrating the victory]]
Within eight minutes, Atlético striker Diego Costa was forced to come off because of his previous injury. [[Gareth Bale]] had Real's best chance just after the half-hour mark and it was only a few minutes later when Atlético punished this miss.<ref name="BBC">{{cite news|title=Real Madrid 4 Atlético Madrid 1|url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/0/football/27383593|access-date=25 May 2014|newspaper=BBC Sport}}</ref> When Atlético received a corner, it was routinely cleared by the Real Madrid defence, but only as far as the head of [[Juanfran (footballer, born 1985)|Juanfran]], who headed the ball back into the penalty area and onto the head of [[Diego Godín]], who caught [[Iker Casillas]] off his line with a looping header to make it 1–0 to Atlético on 36 minutes.<ref name="ESPN FC">{{cite news|title=Real finally secure La Decima|url=http://www.espnfc.com/us/en/report/392450/report.html?soccernet=true&cc=5901|access-date=25 May 2014|newspaper=ESPN FC}}</ref>

The second half saw Real pushing forward as they went in search of an equaliser to deny Atlético's first Champions League title, with manager [[Carlo Ancelotti]] making a double substitution to replace [[Fábio Coentrão]] and Khedira with [[Marcelo (footballer, born 1988)|Marcelo]] and [[Isco]]. Atlético defended with all players and Real missed several chances.<ref name=Yahoo>{{cite news|title=Real Madrid beats Atletico 4–1 in Champions League|url=https://news.yahoo.com/real-madrid-beats-atletico-4-1-champions-league-212736751.html|access-date=25 May 2014|newspaper=Yahoo Sport}}</ref> Atletico's defence was finally breached in the third minute of stoppage time, after the regulation 90 minutes, by a pinpoint [[Sergio Ramos]] header into the left of the net from [[Luka Modrić]]'s corner from the right.<ref name="ESPN FC" /> The match went on to extra time with no substitutions left for either team.

Real Madrid became even more dominant in extra time, which proved decisive after [[Ángel Di María]]'s run on the left flank saw him dribble past three Atlético defenders and shoot at goal. [[Thibaut Courtois]] attempted to block the Argentine's shot but only managed to deflect the ball to Bale, who headed the rebound in from two yards out to put Real ahead for the first time, in the 110th minute.<ref name="BBC" /> Real then added two late goals to the scoreline. [[Cristiano Ronaldo]] squared the ball to Marcelo, who scored with his left foot from inside the penalty area in the 118th minute. At the end of extra time, Ronaldo was fouled by Gabi for a penalty,<ref name="BBC"/> which he converted into the left corner of the net for a record 17th goal in the tournament. Ronaldo became one of the few players to score and assist in a Champions League final. During the celebration for Ronaldo's goal, Varane kicked the ball towards Atlético manager [[Diego Simeone]], who then ran onto the pitch in anger. Simeone was sent off and Varane booked for the incident.<ref name="BBC"/>

This was the seventh UEFA Champions League final (not including the finals up to 1992, when it was known as the European Champion Clubs' Cup) to go into extra time and the first to be solved during this period, without the need for a [[Penalty shoot-out (association football)|penalty shoot-out]]. It was also the second highest-scoring final in the tournament's history as the Champions League, after the 3–3 draw between Milan and [[Liverpool F.C.|Liverpool]] in [[2005 UEFA Champions League final|2005]], and provided the second biggest winning margin, behind Milan's 4–0 win over [[FC Barcelona|Barcelona]] in [[1994 UEFA Champions League final|1994]].<ref name="facts and figures">{{cite web |first=Mike |last=Hammond |title=UEFA Champions League final facts and figures |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0250-0c510961ce15-2f139c4da8cc-1000--uefa-champions-league-final-facts-and-figures/ |publisher=UEFA.com (Union of European Football Associations) |date=25 May 2014 |access-date=25 May 2014 }}</ref>

